---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Eyebrow from "@/components/Eyebrow.astro";
import LabelledBand from "@/components/LabelledBand.astro";

/**
 * The claim the footer makes, written out.
 *
 * Short on purpose. A statement is read by someone who has already hit a
 * problem, so it says what was built, what is weak and how to report it, and
 * stops. The reasoning behind each measure lives in CLAUDE.md, which is where
 * anyone changing the code will look; a visitor wants the list.
 *
 * The limitations are named rather than implied. This site's rule is that an
 * honest absence beats a confident-looking blank, and that has to apply to its
 * own workings first.
 */
const body = "text-body text-ink-muted max-w-[760px] space-y-5";
const list = "text-body text-ink-muted max-w-[760px] list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 marker:text-ink-faint";
---

<Base
  title="Accessibility — Dub Digger"
  description="How Dub Digger is built for assistive technology, what was measured against WCAG 2.1 AA, and what is still weak."
  pageType="WebPage"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Accessibility", href: "/accessibility" }]}
>
  <div class="column pt-16 pb-16 md:pt-24">
    <Eyebrow>Accessibility statement</Eyebrow>
    <h1 class="text-name text-ink-strong mt-6 leading-[0.92] font-bold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Accessibility<span class="text-accent">.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lead text-ink-muted mt-10 max-w-[760px]">
      I want Dub Digger to be usable by as many people as possible, including anyone using a screen
      reader or a keyboard on its own. It is designed and built to meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="column pb-20 md:pb-24">
    <LabelledBand label="Where things stand">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          The site was audited against WCAG 2.1 Level AA in August 2026 and the problems it found
          were fixed. I did that testing myself, so treat it as a careful self-assessment rather
          than a certificate: nobody independent has looked at it, and no screen reader user has
          been through it end to end.
        </p>
        <p>It is also a beta and changes often. If something used to work and now does not, tell me.</p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="What is built in">
      <ul class={list}>
        <li>A skip link, so the header, the search field and the navigation can be jumped at once.</li>
        <li>
          Ordered headings and named regions on every page, including a name on each of the two
          navigation bars.
        </li>
        <li>
          Everything works by keyboard, in the order it appears, with focus always visible. The
          phone menu closes on Escape and keeps the keyboard inside it while it is open.
        </li>
        <li>Text clears 4.7:1 against the background, and anything you can click clears 3:1.</li>
        <li>
          The whole site works with JavaScript off: search is a plain form, and the tabs, the sort
          control and "Load more" are links carrying their state in the address.
        </li>
        <li>
          No images in the content, every icon hidden from assistive technology, and a link that
          leaves the site says so before you follow it.
        </li>
        <li>Reduced motion is respected, and you still get the real figures.</li>
      </ul>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="No third-party content">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          This site loads no third-party content, embeds, fonts or trackers, and sets no cookies. So
          there is no consent banner in your way, and no outside widget carrying accessibility
          problems nobody here can fix.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="Where it is still weak">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          The interface is dense on purpose. It is built for someone who reads Discogs pages for
          fun and wants a lot of information per screen, and that is a choice which suits some
          people and not others. Long lists are paged at forty rows to keep that manageable.
        </p>
        <p>
          The corpus text comes from Discogs, written by thousands of volunteers. Artist names,
          biographies and credit roles arrive as they were typed, so their wording, punctuation and
          capitalisation are not mine to make consistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some artist names are very long and set very large. They should reflow rather than
          overflow at any width, but that is the part I would most expect to have missed a case in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="Tell me if something is off">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          If you hit an obstacle using this site, or you want to report an accessibility problem,
          please <a href="mailto:simone.ferraro@nutek.it" data-contact-open class="link-rule"
            >get in touch</a
          >. I will do my best to answer and to fix it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is useful, but not necessary, to know which page you were on, what you were using to
          read it, and what you expected to happen.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-faint font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
          Last reviewed August 2026
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>
  </div>
</Base>
